 :adr/validation {:measured "query-plane value-cid probe, 4 values, one negative control"
                  :route "nbb, no JVM"
                  :base "io-ipld 7caa26c / org-ietf-cbor 6cb47bd / io-multiformats ff67d85 / org-nist-sha2 becdf25 / kotobase 7e12b35"
                  :read-not-measured ["execution-contract key sets" "kotoba-delta implementation status"]}
 :adr/body "## Context\n\nThe owner brought the next iteration of the Unison x Lisp x IPLD proposal. ADR-2609081400 (2026-09-08) measured its CODE half against what already existed and found most of it landed; ADR-2609106000 (today) closed two of the rows that ADR left open. This iteration extends the same principle past code: datom, transaction, query, plan, schema, policy, patch, receipt, name, doc and test all become one kind of content-addressed object -- a `Form` -- with pure S-expression as its single human/agent notation, under six principles:\n\n1. Everything is a Form. 2. Every Form has a CID. 3. Links are CIDs. 4. Schema gives Forms meaning. 5. Evaluation produces Forms from Forms. 6. Names are data, never identity.\n\nThe code half is settled and is not re-argued here. What is new is the CROSS-PLANE claim, and one measurement decides most of it.\n\n## Where each principle already stands\n\nRead at the shas in Reproduce; principle 6 was measured today.\n\n| # | principle | state |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | everything is a Form | PARTLY, and bounded on purpose. `kotoba.value.v1` is the one value model, and its own docstring states the bound: *the codec covers the VALUE position only -- embedding structures (a semantic definition IR node, a prolly-tree leaf, a datom) keep their own envelopes and call this per value* |\n| 2 | every Form has a CID | LANDED at two granularities -- the definition (DefCID payload v2, Unison-shaped, measured in ADR-2609077000) and the value (`kotoba.value.codec/value-cid`). NOT at subexpression granularity, refused with a reason in ADR-2609081400 |\n| 3 | links are CIDs | LANDED. `ipld/link` is the tag-42 link, and kotobase already documents every block's boundary as an incidence relation whose endpoints are those links (README *Data model -- an incidence merkle graph*, ADR-2608201500) |\n| 4 | schema gives meaning | DECIDED, and since today on disk for KIR: `osaho/resources/kotoba/kir/definition-identity.ipldsch`, with the two things IPLD Schema cannot pin named in the file and the shape verdict refusing to answer checkedness (ADR-2609106000) |\n| 5 | evaluation produces Forms | LANDED, and stricter than the proposal asks -- decision 2 |\n| 6 | names are data, never identity | LANDED on the code plane and measured. **FALSE on the query plane** -- decision 3, which is the one new fact this ADR contributes |\n\n## Decision 1 -- the unification lands at the value model, not at one envelope\n\n**A single `Form` envelope shared across planes is refused. What is shared, and what every plane must call, is `kotoba.value.codec`.**\n\nThis workspace has now reached the same shape three times from three directions, and it is the same answer each time: share the layer BELOW, not the layer above.\n\n- ADR-2608039970: what the query surfaces share is the datom plane, not Datalog. Giving every surface one IR was rejected; giving them all one physical model below was not.\n- ADR-2609076000 decision 5: the DefCID seals the LOGICAL form only. Sealing the representation would move every identity on a representational change; letting the encoder vary without sealing it would give one meaning two identities.\n- `ipld.value`: one value codec, and embedding envelopes stay their own.\n\nA shared envelope is the second of those failure modes at the plane level: a datom, a query, a policy and a receipt do not mean the same kind of thing, and forcing one envelope over them either erases what distinguishes them or smuggles the distinguishing field back in as a tag -- which is an envelope again, with less checking. The proposal's real content survives this intact, because the property it wants (notation is not identity; equal values have equal addresses) is a property of the VALUE model, and that is already one thing.\n\nThe rule that follows is small and enforceable by review: **a new plane brings an envelope and calls `kotoba.value.codec` for the values inside it.** An envelope that hand-rolls its own scalar encoding is exactly the finding ADR-2609076000 exists to close, one plane over.\n\n## Decision 2 -- the universal Eval relation already exists, is versioned, and forbids precisely what the proposal would put in it\n\nThe proposal's `(eval (input Q) (evaluator E) (environment D) (output R))` is `kotobase.execution-contract` version 1, three records with exact key sets:\n\n    ExecutionManifest  #{:execution/version :data/commit :authority/policy :authority/epoch\n                         :location/manifest :schema/root :parent :issued-at :signature}\n    RequestEnvelope    #{:request/version :principal :tenant :graph :operation :query/digest\n                         :base/commit :authority/policy :authority/epoch :nonce :expires-at}\n    ExecutionReceipt   #{:receipt/version :request/digest :execution/manifest :query/plan-digest\n                         :authority/decision :result/root :cost :implementation/build :signature}\n\nThree properties of it are worth naming because the proposal reinvents two of them and would undo the third.\n\n**It separates semantic identity from physical plan.** `:query/digest` in the envelope and `:query/plan-digest` in the receipt are different fields, which is the proposal's *never give a query and its plan the same CID*, already contractual.\n\n**It refuses to carry syntax.** `forbidden-keys` rejects `:query/text`, `:query/ast`, `:query/protocol`, `:protocol`, `:backend`, `:provider`, `:credential`, `:token`, `:authorization`. The record names addresses; the AST is what an adapter translates FROM, and `execution-contract`'s docstring says adapters may translate their own ASTs into these records but may not add protocol-specific fields to the signed contract. A `Form` that carried the query's own S-expression into the receipt would put back the field the contract exists to keep out.\n\n**Its identifiers are computed, not accepted.** `bind-digest!` recomputes the envelope's digest and rejects a mismatch, on the stated ground that an envelope is evidence about a query nobody ran unless the digest is one anybody can re-derive; `kotobase.execution-identity` names the one codec (`kotoba.value.codec/value-cid`) once. And `:location/manifest` sits beside `:schema/root` rather than inside identity -- the same identity/naming/location separation ADR-2609092600 draws for the app plane, at a different layer.\n\n**So: do not add an `(eval ...)` Form. Bring a new plane onto these records.** A second answer to *what is one evaluation* is the shape decision 1 refuses, and this one is already signed, versioned and fail-closed.\n\n## Decision 3 -- names are outside identity on the code plane and INSIDE it on the query plane (measured 2026-09-10)\n\nPrinciple 6 is true where it was built and false where it was assumed. Four `kotoba.value.codec/value-cid` addresses of the same kotobase-dialect query, one per row:\n\n| probe | value | address |\n|---|---|---|\n| A one line | `[:find ?e :where [?e \"score\" ?s] [(>= ?s 25)]]` | `bafyreifdssv5uyammwfzdcgfse335hfmy7dwwnippfn3bveakfd2v4aif4` |\n| A re-indented over five lines | same value, different notation | **identical** |\n| B negative control, 25 -> 30 | | `bafyreid7zlhedza3tfqx7cr2fu2qmhjmsmgiczisjrohexkh2evxkvqzke` |\n| C `?e ?s` renamed to `?entity ?score` | same query, renamed logic variables | `bafyreihcgldobe6am4pvakh2ivv5n62bwnhqgl3h6pwte5lls63ztf46d4` |\n\nA and its re-indentation agree, so the proposal's *notation is not identity* already holds for queries with no new machinery -- an EDN query is a value, and whitespace was never in it. B moves, so the agreement in A is discriminating rather than degenerate. **C moves too.** On the code plane an equivalent edit -- rename the binder, its parameter and its call site -- leaves the DefCID byte-identical (ADR-2609077000 property 2), because binders are renamed to de Bruijn positions before sealing. The query plane has no such pass: `?e` and `?entity` are two symbols, so they are two values, so they are two addresses.\n\nThat is not a latent detail. `:query/digest` IS this address, computed by `bind-digest!`. **Two alpha-equivalent queries therefore mint two RequestEnvelopes, two ExecutionReceipts, and a miss in anything keyed on the digest.**\n\n**This ADR does not call it a defect and does not fix it.** Both readings are defensible: the digest names the query as it was submitted, which is what an auditor re-derives, and per-request records were never meant to be shared between requesters. What is not defensible is leaving the asymmetry unwritten, because principle 6 reads as a property of the system and is a property of one plane.\n\nThe cost of changing it is known in shape, from the neighbouring case: normalizing query identity moves every digest that has ever been stored or signed, the same way adopting `kotoba.value.v1` in the DefCID payload would move every frozen vector and module lock (ADR-2609076000 decision 6). That is an owner decision with a version bump attached, not a tidy-up.\n\nAnd it has a precondition that is load-bearing in its own right. To normalize `?e` away, something must know which symbols are BINDING positions -- which means reading the clause structure rather than the value. Today the clause is a positional triple; ADR-2608201500 records that role, sign and mult are erased at exactly that step and that **erasing labels is not reversible**. So query-identity normalization and the labelled-boundary generalization are the same piece of work approached from two sides, and the order in which they are done is not a matter of taste.\n\n## Decision 4 -- per-node identity stays refused, and the reason extends to the data planes\n\nADR-2609081400 refused a CID for every parenthesised node, on the ground that a subterm under a binder is an OPEN term and content-addressing it naively is unsound. That argument is about binders, so it does not transfer verbatim to a datom, which is closed and already has an address as a block. The refusal still holds for a different reason: giving every `()` an address ACROSS planes creates a second identity space over the same meanings, which is decision 5 of ADR-2609076000 restated. The proposal's actual use -- an agent that edits by naming a node instead of regenerating a file -- needs a PATH into an already-sealed block, and `ipld.selector` / `ipld.graph` already produce bounded traversals with CAR/GraphSync proof blocks.\n\n## Decision 5 -- the name `Form` is not available in this workspace\n\nTwo live meanings already hold it. `kotoba-lang/forms` is an office product with a Pages editor and a `:forms/form` resource kind in the shared Kotoba office envelope. And *forms* is the existing name for reader output in the compiler pipeline that `kotoba-sema` documents as `source bytes -> forms -> semantic analysis -> checked kotoba-hir`. Naming the cross-plane object `Form` would make `forms.wire/form-envelope` and *the form plane* the same word for unrelated things, in a workspace whose naming rule already says a name that does not indicate its function must announce itself in its README and register in `manifest/concept-vocabulary.edn`.\n\nThe planes already have names -- value, definition, datom, commit, query, plan, manifest, receipt, grant -- and decision 1 means there is no new object that needs one.\n\n## What the proposal is right about and is not built\n\n**Schema is addressed but not queryable.** `:schema/root` is a field of the signed ExecutionManifest, so a schema already HAS an address in the record that governs an execution. What does not exist is a query surface that answers *does Person have an email field* from that address. The proposal's schema-as-datoms is that missing half, and it is buildable without inventing an identity, because the identity is already in the manifest.\n\n**The labelled boundary.** The proposal's closing move -- read `(f a b)` as an incidence relation with labelled endpoints rather than a head-plus-arguments syntax -- is the accepted framing in kotobase's data model already, and ADR-2608201500 records exactly where it stops: the query layer's clauses are positional triples, so role, sign and mult have nowhere to sit. That is the one item on this list where waiting has a cost, per decision 3.\n\n**Patch and derivation as data -- and the first answer given here was the wrong layer.** This ADR originally answered the proposal's `(patch (base CID-A) (replace CID-X CID-Y))` with `kotoba-delta` (ADR-2607161325). Measured: `delta.op`'s operations are `:write`, `:remove` and `:edit` over FILES, where `:op/old` must occur exactly once as a text substring (Edit-tool semantics), and `delta.anchor` anchors to a top-level definition by (kind, name) plus a content hash of its TEXT, using a balanced-delimiter splitter that its own docstring says is not the reader. That is a signed op-log over source text with a text-derived anchor. It is not an operation over a Data Model path, and it is not `(replace old-cid new-cid)`.\n\nIts own *does NOT decide* list already names what is missing -- an S-expression structural anchor, definition CID plus form path -- written 2026-07-16, before the DefCID existed in the shape it now has. So the gap is recorded and its precondition has since landed.\n\n## Measured against the IPLD spec index (2026-09-10)\n\nThe owner asked for this arrangement to be checked against the published IPLD specification surface before going further. Most of that surface was checked once already: **ADR-2608170400** (2026-08-17) is this workspace's coverage pass over Data Model, codecs, selectors, Schema/DMT/ADL, CARv2, IPNS, transports and GraphSync, and it carries the numbered gaps. `io-ipld` implements `core` (DAG-CBOR), `dag_json`, `dag_pb`, `data_model`, `link`, `schema_dsl`, `schema`, `selector`, `graph` (bounded traversal and trustless pathing), `fbl` and `value`, with a DAG-CBOR canonical/invalid corpus and a selector-kind coverage file that records the two non-adopted kinds as decisions rather than omissions. CARv1 and CARv2 are `io-ipld-car`.\n\nFour items on the index are not covered there. Two of them matter to this proposal.\n\n| index item | state here, measured 2026-09-10 |\n|---|---|\n| **Patch** (with `fixtures-1`) | **was absent and never named; built 2026-09-10** -- see *What was built* below. Before that: no repo in `manifest/west.yml`, no namespace in `io-ipld`, not in the concept vocabulary, and ADR-2608170400 contained the string zero times |\n| **HAMT ADL** (with HashMap fixtures) | absent as an ADL -- `fbl` is the only one implemented. The word appears twice in the record and means something different each time: ADR-2608170400 warns against reading a HAMT as an ordered query index, and ADR-2609021600 plans a HAMT as a *language* collection written in Kotoba. Neither is the IPLD HashMap ADL |\n| **DAG-JOSE / DAG-ETH / DAG-COSMOS** | absent, and that is a decision rather than a gap -- these are domain codecs for planes this workspace does not have. Recorded so the absence stops being unexamined |\n| **Design / Tricky Choices** (`ordering`, `numeric-domain`, `map-key-domain`, `string-domain`) | not named anywhere in the record, and they are precisely the axes on which our own two encoders measurably disagree: tag alphabet, scalar payload, and map key order (ADR-2609076000, ten frozen vectors, all ten disagreeing). **payload v3 is still an open owner decision, and it should be argued against the upstream positions on those axes, not only against our two implementations** |\n| **Open Research: ADL autoexecution** | upstream this is open research. Here the equivalent boundary is deliberately CLOSED -- `ipld-adl-wasm-v1`, transform module pinned by raw CID, fuel and output-byte and memory budgets declared by the caller, no ambient filesystem, network, clock or randomness. Keep it closed: autoexecution puts the choice of interpreter inside the data, and a caller-declared capability is the opposite of that |\n\nThe Patch row is the one to act on, and it is cheap for a reason worth stating: the operations are over paths, `ipld.graph/resolve-path` already returns a selected value with a root-first proof, and the spec ships fixtures. What it needs is a decision about which plane it serves -- an agent editing a sealed definition graph is not the same problem as `kotoba-delta`'s agent editing files -- and that decision is not taken here.\n\n## What was built, 2026-09-10 -- `ipld.patch`, in Kotoba\n\nThe Patch row above is the one this ADR said to act on, and it is now landed in\n`kotoba-lang/io-ipld` (merged `8375715c`, west pin advanced by `9ac5d933`). The\nowner's instruction was to write it in Kotoba, so the shape is:\n\n| | `kotoba/ipld/patch.kotoba` | `src/ipld/patch.cljc` |\n|---|---|---|\n| role | the implementation | the oracle, kept per ADR-2608261000 |\n| plane | `:document` -- null, bool, i64, f64, string, keyword, symbol, map, vector, list, set | the full Data Model, with `:bytes` and `:link` |\n| errors | `[:result :document :keyword]`, never a control effect | `ex-info` carrying `:problem` |\n| runs on | restricted ESM and wasm32-browser, both executed | JVM and nbb |\n\n**The `:document` op set turned out to be exactly the one Patch needs** --\n`document-assoc` / `document-dissoc` / `document-contains` / `document-get` for\nmaps, `document-vector-assoc` / `-conj` / `-remove` / `-at` for lists,\n`document-equal?` for `test`. Map keys admit `:keyword` **or `:document`**, so\nan IPLD map's string keys carry over unchanged. Nothing was added to the\nlanguage and no capability is required: the component compiles with an empty\n`requiredCapabilities` and an empty effect row.\n\n**Measured, executed rather than built.** `main` returns the COUNT of failing\nchecks, not a boolean, because a boolean cannot tell one regression from a\nbroken build. All eight upstream fixtures and nine decision checks return 0\nthrough the restricted-ESM target and again through `wasm32-browser` -- two\nruntimes, one instance per check because fuel is 512 per instance and is not a\nknob. Both halves discriminate: removing the leading-zero rule reddens exactly\n`d3`, making `move` behave as `copy` reddens exactly `f6`, and restoring is\nbyte-identical. The oracle is 18 tests / 48 assertions under nbb, and comparing\nbytes with `=` instead of by content reddens exactly the byte-equality test;\nthe whole `io-ipld` nbb suite is 140 tests / 5630 assertions.\n\n**Four things the spec leaves open are decided fail-closed**, with the reason in\nthe module header rather than only in a commit message: paths do not cross\nlinks (upstream `fixtures-1` covers single blocks and says so; the multi-block\nfixtures do not exist yet), `~0`/`~1` are not unescaped (adopting the RFC 6902\nrule silently would make the same patch name a different key here than in an\nimplementation that read the spec literally -- one patch, two documents), list\nindices are canonical decimal (without it `/01` and `/1` address one element\ntwo ways), and `move` refuses to move a node into its own child.\n\n**Three limits are stated rather than glossed.** Native `aarch64-macos` refuses\nthis component -- *typed values currently require the kotoba-script web target,\ntyped Wasm target, or qualified native string/scalar-record/option-i64/\nresult-i64 features*. That is a backend gap, not a language ceiling\n(ADR-2608650000's classification), and it is written in the module header as\nsuch. The `:document` plane has no `:bytes` and no `:link`, so the Kotoba half\ncovers the EDN-shaped subset of the Data Model and the two refusals only the\nfull model can express -- a path crossing a link, and an ADL substrate reached\nby a path -- live in the oracle. And a patch has no CID on the Kotoba side:\nCIDv1 assembly and DAG-CBOR are `.cljc` layers, and `document-sha256` is a\ndigest of a document, not a CID. Calling it one would give a value two\naddresses, which is the thing decision 1 of this ADR exists to prevent.\n\n### Which surface it is written in, and the ceiling that decides it\n\nCorrected 2026-09-10 after the owner asked whether Kotoba's syntax is the pure\nS-expression grammar rather than a Clojure-shaped one. It is, and the module\nwas in the wrong spelling for its extension: `.kotoba` is the **canonical pure\nS-expression core** and `.cljk` is the Clojure-shaped surface\n(kotoba-lang `ADR-kotoba-pure-s-expression-core-and-cljk-surface`, and\n`kotoba-core-contracts` `resources/kotoba/lang/source_contract.edn`, where\n`.kotoba` is the only source kind marked `:canonical? true`).\n\nRewritten: every call to a definition the module defines is now\n`(app (ref f) ...)`. Builtin calls stay plain prefix application, which that\nADR admits as core -- application is `(app f args...)` OR `(f args...)`.\n\n**The rewrite was verified by identity rather than by reading it.** That ADR's\nsection 3 says both surfaces mint the same Definition CID for equivalent\nnormalized semantics; here all **25** Definition CIDs are byte-identical across\nthe two spellings, which is a 25-definition module against the three pairs the\nADR itself measured. The comparison discriminates: changing one digit\ncomparison moves ten CIDs -- the edited definition and its nine transitive\ndependents -- and the 8 fixtures plus 9 decision checks still return 0 through\nrestricted ESM and again through wasm32-browser.\n\nTwo limits of the pure core were measured on the way (amu `b57bcdb6`), and both\nare implementation state rather than design:\n\n- **`(def name (lam [params] body))` is refused.** That is the definition form\n  the ADR names for the pure surface; `def` takes a *closed bounded\n  integer/string/keyword/boolean/nil* constant. So `defn` is the only admitted\n  definition form in **either** surface today, and `defn` in a `.kotoba` file\n  is not that file falling back to `.cljk`.\n- **`(ref name)` names only a definition the module itself defines.** Pointed\n  at a builtin it is refused with *every other shape of `ref` is Clojure's STM\n  constructor, and state must not be ambient*.\n\n**Nothing enforces the split.** The Clojure-shaped spelling compiled, checked,\nminted CIDs and ran under the canonical extension for a full day before anyone\nlooked at the extension. A detector for that is not written here.\n\n### The third patch plane, found while building this\n\n`kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang` `lang/definition-patch.edn` is `:status :implemented`\nwith `:ops #{:add :replace :remove}` over **name-to-DefCID bindings** -- a\nnamespace patch in the Unison sense, whose unit is explicitly\n`:definition-cid-ops-and-name-mappings` and explicitly `:not` a source-file\ncopy or a source-tree CID.\n\nSo the workspace has patch at three planes now, and they are not versions of\neach other:\n\n| plane | what an operation names | where |\n|---|---|---|\n| text | a file, and a substring that must occur exactly once | `kotoba-lang/kotoba-delta` |\n| name binding | a name, and the DefCID it points at | `kotoba-lang` `lang/definition-patch.edn` |\n| **value** | a path into a document | **`io-ipld` `ipld.patch`, new** |\n\nThe proposal's `(replace old-cid new-cid)` wants the third, and reaching a\nsealed definition through it is still not possible: paths do not cross links\nhere, and `definition-patch.edn` lists `:patch-document-cid` among its own\n`:unmeasured`. Naming the three planes is what this section adds; joining them\nis not decided anywhere yet.\n\n## What is NOT claimed\n- The Kotoba component was executed on two runtimes; it was NOT executed on native, which refuses it, and NOT compared against the `.cljc` oracle by running both over one shared corpus. The two suites assert the same fixtures separately.\n\n- The probe measured the kotobase dialect only. The SQL, SPARQL, Cypher and GraphQL surfaces were not measured, and one of them may normalize its variables.\n- No claim that normalizing query identity is desirable. The claim is that the asymmetry exists, has a measurable consequence at `:query/digest`, and is currently unwritten.\n- The key sets and `forbidden-keys` were READ from source at the sha below. No live governed execution was run.\n- `kotoba-delta`'s implementation status was read from its ADR, not measured.\n- Nothing here is a gate. No check asserts that a new plane's envelope goes through `kotoba.value.codec`, and this ADR does not invent one.\n\n## Reproduce\n\nDecision 3, at `io-ipld` `7caa26c`, `org-ietf-cbor` `6cb47bd`, `io-multiformats` `ff67d85`, `org-nist-sha2` `becdf25`, `text` `73bdb13`-pinned checkout -- each 0 behind its last-fetched default branch, each clean:\n\n    nbb --classpath \"orgs/kotoba-lang/io-ipld/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/org-ietf-cbor/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/io-multiformats/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/org-nist-sha2/src:orgs/kotoba-lang/text/src\" <probe>\n\nwhere the probe prints `(vc/value-cid q)` for the four values in the table above. Keep the re-indented pair AND the 25 -> 30 control: a comparison that only ever reports agreement cannot be told from one that never ran. `kotoba.lang.text` must be on the classpath or `io-ipld` will not load, which is a missing dependency rather than a codec answer.\n\nDecisions 2 and 5, at `kotobase` `7e12b35` and `ayatori` `91bcd02`:\n\n    sed -n '1,45p' orgs/kotoba-lang/kotobase/src/kotobase/execution_contract.cljc\n    sed -n '170,190p' orgs/kotoba-lang/kotobase/src/kotobase/governed_execution.cljc\n    sed -n '1,15p'  orgs/kotoba-lang/forms/README.md\n\n## Session close, 2026-09-10 — what landed, what is open, where to resume\n\nEverything below is on a default branch; nothing of this session's is left on a\nbranch, in a worktree, or in a stash.\n\n| landed | where |\n|---|---|\n| this arrangement, and its two corrections (the IPLD spec index; the surface) | `com-junkawasaki/root` main |\n| `ipld.patch` — six operations, `.kotoba` pure spelling + `.cljc` oracle | `kotoba-lang/io-ipld` `f2d04876`, west pin `f2d04876` |\n| q9 `:wasm32-lowering-measured` and `:pure-spelling-in-production` | `kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang` `5e207158`, contained in pin `38b98e0f` |\n| detector `verify-printed-value-identity` + registration (85th) | root main, `manifest/orgs-detectors.edn` |\n| character-literal fix in two scanners, and ADR-2609108000 | root main |\n\n**Verification actually run**, rather than cited: IPLD Patch's 8 upstream\nfixtures and 9 decision checks return 0 through restricted ESM **and** through\nwasm32-browser, one instance per check; the `.cljc` oracle is 18 tests / 48\nassertions under nbb inside a suite of 140 tests / 5,630 assertions; the pure\nrewrite holds all 25 Definition CIDs byte-identical with a control that moves\nten; the new detector swept 27,823 files for 64 findings and five opened at\nrandom were all the shape; the scanner fix leaves the corpus identical\n(`SCANNED 119`, `FINDINGS 4`) and is a latent fix, not a behaviour change.\nEvery one of those has a control that was run in the failing direction.\n\n**Open, in the order they are worth doing.**\n\n1. **Eight hand-written scanners are UNMEASURED** for the character-literal\n   inversion — two-argument signatures the paired probe could not call. They\n   are not clean; nobody has run the probe against them. ADR-2609108000 lists\n   them by file.\n2. **Nothing enforces the `.kotoba` surface.** The Clojure-shaped spelling\n   compiled, checked, minted CIDs and ran under the canonical extension for a\n   day. q9 step 3 — the profile as an enforced admission gate — is the language\n   decision that gates a detector here, so a detector should not be written\n   before it.\n3. **The 64 printed-value findings are unread.** Each says *measure whether the\n   printed text or the value is the artifact*. `akashi/cid.cljc` (a Datom log)\n   and `aratame/ledger.cljc` (a hash-chained ledger) are the two to read first.\n4. **`rel` and `query` do not lower to wasm32** (`invalid Wasm index operand`).\n   Recorded; not investigated.\n5. **Nothing checks decision 1 in the other direction** — that a new plane's\n   envelope